# Site-data scan findings to accept, one `<blob-sha>:<finding class>` per line, each with its reason.
#
# ⚠️ **This file is the wrong tool for real site data.** If a hostname or address a site is actually
# reachable at is ever committed, the answer is the destructive, owner-coordinated history work
# SECURITY.md describes — never a line here. An entry is only ever appropriate for a value that was
# never sensitive, and the comment above it has to establish that.
#
# ⚠️ **And it cannot quote the values it is about.** This file is scanned like any other, so naming
# a refused address here would make the scan refuse this file — measured, on the first draft. The
# reasons below therefore name *ranges by their RFC* and describe the rest. That is the same
# constraint `geofeed.ts` and `history-leak-scan.test.ts` already work under.
#
# ## Why it has to exist
#
# `scan-public-history.mjs --all` reads every reachable **blob**, and blobs are immutable. Fixing
# the tree does not remove them, so one false positive in a merged commit makes the full-history
# scan fail for ever — and an alarm that can never be cleared is one that stops being read. That is
# not hypothetical here: every scheduled run `trusted-leaks.yml` ever had (2026-08-25, 2026-09-01)
# failed on the four entries below, while the pull-request gate — which only reads *introduced*
# commits — went on passing. The half `ci.yml` calls authoritative was the half nobody could see.
#
# `.gitleaksignore` exists in this repository for exactly the same reason and says so in its own
# header. This is the site-data axis's equivalent.
#
# ## Keyed by blob, not by commit
#
# A blob belongs to every commit whose tree contains it, so commit-keyed entries would need one line
# per commit and would grow with every merge. The seven refusals below are four blobs.
#
# To generate candidate lines: `node scripts/scan-public-history.mjs --all --print-accept-lines`.
# It prints object ids and finding classes only — never a matched value. An entry that stops
# matching anything makes `--all` fail, so this file cannot quietly rot into folklore.
#
# ## ⚠️ A local `--all` is not the scan CI runs
#
# `--all` is `git rev-list --all`, which walks **every ref in the clone you are standing in** —
# including other remotes. Measured here: a local run reported 722 refusals across 219 commits, and
# **every one of those commits was unreachable from `origin/main`**; they came in over a second
# remote sharing this object database. CI checks out this repository alone, so its `--all` sees
# only what is reachable from the default branch.
#
# So a red local `--all` may be about a repository that is not this one — the same trap as the
# `policy/` symlink in AGENTS.md, wearing different clothes. To reproduce what CI actually does,
# scan a clean single-branch clone rather than your working checkout.

# ── The 2026-08-24 renderer commits (19bf9b9, 296e287) ────────────────────────
#
# Three files, all fixtures and comments. `aa2efae` — "누출 스캐너가 결함을 설명하는 글을 막고
# 있었다 — CI 는 3커밋 동안 빨갰다" — rewrote the literals so the *tree* would pass, which is why
# the current worktree scan is clean. The blobs stayed, and `--all` reads blobs.
#
# I read all three blobs. Every literal the current tree no longer carries is one of:
#
#   · documentation space (RFC 5737 for v4, RFC 3849 for v6), RFC 1918 private space, loopback,
#     link-local or multicast — the reserved ranges these very suites exist to test the handling of;
#   · a deliberately malformed address in a test that refuses it — an octet above 255, and an
#     octet with a leading zero that some parsers read as octal;
#   · the two IANA global-unicast floor prefixes, in the tests that pin the floor. They are public
#     by definition, which is the whole point of the assertion;
#   · one well-known public **anycast resolver** prefix, used as the known positive. Its own comment
#     says why it is there: "Without it every test above passes against a parser that refuses all
#     IPv6." A known positive has to be a real global address or it proves nothing;
#   · a v4 address and its hexadecimal v6-compatible spelling, inside a comment in `geofeed.ts`
#     explaining a case the scanner does **not** detect. Refusing to let a defect be written down
#     is precisely the failure `aa2efae` was named for.
#
# None is an address any site of ours is reachable at. The scanner cannot tell an example address
# from a real one, and it should not try — that is what this file, with a human reason attached, is
# for.

